## **HERMES vs OPENCLAW — THE COMMUNITY DEBATE**

This is the comparison the community keeps coming back to. Here are the most substantive takes:

**The architectural argument (for Hermes):**

@MartinSzerment: "AI agents as we know them are already obsolete. The industry still treats skills and memory as two separate problems. Hermes builds and refines its own skills autonomously. OpenClaw still relies on human-written and tuned skills. Hermes runs a stacked memory system." [https://x.com/MartinSzerment/status/2041017839297990902](https://x.com/MartinSzerment/status/2041017839297990902) 

@alcazarsec (retweeted by Teknium): "Hermes is the more interesting design if you want an agent that builds memory and reusable skills as it works." [https://x.com/alcazarsec/status/2037325294764494875](https://x.com/alcazarsec/status/2037325294764494875) 

**The migration pattern:**

@sudoingX: "my timeline is full of these now. openclaw bloat users migrating to hermes agent on their own. the pattern is always the same. slow gateway, broken tool calls on small local models, generic parsing that blames your model for bad output. then they try hermes and everything works." [https://x.com/sudoingX/status/2039929603863855556](https://x.com/sudoingX/status/2039929603863855556) 

**The balanced take:**

@KSimback (retweeted by Teknium): "I was impressed with Hermes agent from @NousResearch within 5 minutes of install. But my main reservation with it vs OpenClaw..." [https://x.com/KSimback/status/2035871657462751391](https://x.com/KSimback/status/2035871657462751391) 

**The counterpoint:**

@ksubedi: "After testing the Hermes agent for a while, I'm going back to OpenClaw. Seems like Hermes has a lot of edge case issues." [https://x.com/ksubedi/status/2039386353688104989](https://x.com/ksubedi/status/2039386353688104989) 

